---

<PaperLayout slug="judicial-arms-race" aims={aims} sections={sections}>
  <!-- 01 -->
  <section class="rsec" id="puzzle">
    <p class="r-eyebrow">01 · The puzzle</p>
    <h2>A tiny state that runs corporate America</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Delaware’s grip on incorporations rests on one institution: the Court of Chancery, a specialist business court with
      no juries, expert judges and two centuries of case law. In 2023–24 that court itself became the problem.
    </p>
    <div class="r-stats">
      <div class="r-stat"><span class="n">0.3%</span><span class="l">of Americans live in Delaware</span></div>
      <div class="r-stat"><span class="n">68%</span><span class="l">of Fortune 500 companies incorporate there</span></div>
      <div class="r-stat"><span class="n">≈ ⅓</span><span class="l">of the state’s general fund comes from franchise taxes and fees</span></div>
      <div class="r-stat"><span class="n">1792</span><span class="l">Court of Chancery founded</span></div>
    </div>
    <p>
      States used to compete for companies by rewriting statutes. The paper argues something new is happening: states now
      compete by <em>redesigning their courts</em>. Not forum shopping, where litigants choose among fixed courts, but <strong>forum creation</strong>, where courts are built as products.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- 02 -->
  <section class="rsec" id="theory">
    <p class="r-eyebrow">02 · Theory</p>
    <h2>Leave, stay, or threaten to leave</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Competitive federalism starts from Tiebout and Hirschman: an unhappy firm can exit to another state, use its voice to
      demand change, or stay loyal. The paper models this as a game.
    </p>

    <Fig title="Exit, voice and loyalty as a game" hint="Tap an ending to read it" caption="Redrawn from Figure 1. Payoffs are (firm, state). E is the value of exit, c the cost of voice, L the loyalty payoff.">
      <div class="evln" data-evln>
        <div class="node chooser">Firm</div>
        <ul class="branches">
          <li>
            <span class="edge">Exit</span>
            <button class="leaf" data-k="exit" type="button"><b>{evln.exit.pay}</b></button>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="edge">Voice</span>
            <div class="node chooser gov">State</div>
            <ul class="branches">
              <li>
                <span class="edge">Respond</span>
                <button class="leaf" data-k="respond" type="button"><b>{evln.respond.pay}</b></button>
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="edge">Ignore</span>
                <div class="node chooser">Firm</div>
                <ul class="branches">
                  <li><span class="edge">Exit</span><button class="leaf" data-k="ignoreExit" type="button"><b>{evln.ignoreExit.pay}</b></button></li>
                  <li><span class="edge">Loyalty</span><button class="leaf" data-k="ignoreLoyal" type="button"><b>{evln.ignoreLoyal.pay}</b></button></li>
                </ul>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="edge">Loyalty</span>
            <button class="leaf" data-k="loyalty" type="button"><b>{evln.loyalty.pay}</b></button>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="evln-read" aria-live="polite">{evln.respond.read}</p>
        <script is:inline type="application/json" data-evln-text set:html={JSON.stringify(Object.fromEntries(Object.entries(evln).map(([k, v]) => [k, v.read])))} />
      </div>
    </Fig>

    <p>
      Exit only disciplines a state if leaving is credible. For decades it wasn’t, because any rival that innovated walked
      into a trap.
    </p>

    <Fig title="Delaware’s “stalking horse” trap" hint="Tap a step" caption="Redrawn from Figure 3 (after Bebchuk & Hamdani, 2002). The dashed return marks the loop that kept rivals out.">
      <ol class="horse" data-steps>
        {
          horse.map(([t, d], i) => (
            <li>
              <button type="button" aria-pressed={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'} data-d={d}>
                <span class="hn">{i + 1}</span>
                <span class="ht">{t}</span>
              </button>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
      <p class="horse-read" aria-live="polite">{horse[0][1]}</p>
      <div class="horse-back" aria-hidden="true"><span>back to the start: Delaware keeps its lead</span></div>
    </Fig>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 -->
  <section class="rsec" id="loop">
    <p class="r-eyebrow">03 · Method</p>
    <h2>A loop with no exit</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      The paper uses process tracing across Delaware, Texas and Nevada, the only states that answered DExit by redesigning
      courts. It follows a four-stage cycle in time order rather than state by state, so the chain of cause and effect stays
      visible.
    </p>

    <Fig title="The competitive feedback loop" hint="Tap a stage to jump to it" caption="Redrawn from Figure 2. The cycle begins from a stable equilibrium (dashed); once disrupted, each defensive response re-enters as a new disruption.">
      <div class="loop">
        <svg class="loop-ring" viewBox="0 0 200 200" aria-hidden="true">
          <defs>
            <marker id="ah" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="7" refY="5" markerWidth="5" markerHeight="5" orient="auto">
              <path d="M0 0 10 5 0 10z" fill="currentColor" />
            </marker>
          </defs>
          <path d="M137.1 40.6 A70 70 0 0 1 163.9 71.5" class="seg" marker-end="url(#ah)" />
          <path d="M163.9 128.5 A70 70 0 0 1 137.1 159.4" class="seg" marker-end="url(#ah)" />
          <path d="M62.9 159.4 A70 70 0 0 1 36.1 128.5" class="seg" marker-end="url(#ah)" />
          <path d="M36.1 71.5 A70 70 0 0 1 62.9 40.6" class="seg" marker-end="url(#ah)" />
        </svg>
        <span class="eq">Stable equilibrium</span>
        <a class="st s1" href="#stage-1"><span class="rn">I</span>Competitive disruption</a>
        <a class="st s2" href="#stage-2"><span class="rn">II</span>Elite signalling</a>
        <a class="st s3" href="#stage-3"><span class="rn">III</span>Challengers emerge</a>
        <a class="st s4" href="#stage-4"><span class="rn">IV</span>Defensive response</a>
      </div>
    </Fig>

    <div class="r-cards c3">
      <div class="r-card"><h4>Statutes</h4><p>What changed, provision by provision, matched against the grievances behind DExit.</p></div>
      <div class="r-card"><h4>Legislative records</h4><p>Why it changed: the timing, and the competitive reasons politicians gave.</p></div>
      <div class="r-card"><h4>Law-firm advisories</h4><p>How it landed: the tone of four elite firms tracked through every stage.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 -->
  <section class="rsec" id="stage-1">
    <p class="r-eyebrow">04 · Stage I · Competitive disruption</p>
    <h2>Six rulings that broke the spell</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      After Kathleen McCormick became Chancellor in 2021, the court’s rulings turned against long-standing market practice.
      Five of these six landmark rulings came within two months.
    </p>

    <Fig title="Landmark Chancery rulings, 2023–24" hint="Tap a ruling for what it held" replay caption="From Table 3. Dollar figures as stated in the paper.">
      <div class="rtl" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="rtl-axis">
          {['Jan 2023', 'Jul 2023', 'Jan 2024'].map((l, i) => <span style={`left:${(i * 6 / 13) * 100}%`}>{l}</span>)}
        </div>
        {
          rulingDots.map((r) => (
            <span
              class="rtl-dot a-pop"
              style={`left:${(r.m / 13) * 100}%;bottom:${8 + r.k * 18}px;--i:${r.i}`}
              data-tip={`${r.name}|${r.date}`}
            />
          ))
        }
        <span class="rtl-brace" style={`left:${(12 / 13) * 100}%`}>5 in 2 months</span>
      </div>
      <div class="rlist">
        {
          rulings.map((r) => (
            <details class="rrow">
              <summary>
                <span class="rd">{r.date}</span>
                <span class="rn2">{r.name}</span>
                {r.tag && <span class="r-chip">{r.tag}</span>}
              </summary>
              <div class="rbody">
